       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the problem is clinical and not an authorization issue. 50 mg of amlodipine is five times the maximum daily dose, and it could seriously harm the patient. The order needs to come from the treating prescriber, not from an administrator confirming it.\n\nHere are options that would let this move forward:\n- **The prescriber enters the order themselves.** If they really intend 50 mg, pharmacy review and their own clinical judgment are the right safeguards. I shouldn't bypass those.\n- **I enter a standard dose.** If the intent is to treat hypertension or angina, I can enter amlodipine 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily. The prescriber should tell me which one.\n- **The prescriber or pharmacist contacts me.** If there's a clinical reason for the high dose, they can document it and place the order through the proper channels.\n\nIf the patient needs blood pressure treatment now, please get the treating clinician involved. Tell me the dose they choose and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
